          "specific_feedback": "Your answer repeats terms like \u201cinteraction\u201d and mixes up which layer does what, making the explanation hard to follow.",
          "improvement": "Try: \"The presentation layer delivers the SAP GUI to the user; the application layer handles business logic and processes requests; the database layer stores and retrieves the data needed for those requests.\"",
          "next_step": "Keep building on this foundation, and focus on concise, distinct explanations for each component in the next question.",
          "provider": "groq"
        },
        "model_answer": "Sure, let me break it down the way I usually explain it. An SAP system is built on a three\u2011tier architecture: the presentation layer, the application layer, and the database layer. The presentation layer is what the end\u2011user actually sees \u2013 the SAP GUI, Fiori launchpad, or any web\u2011based UI \u2013 and it handles the screen rendering and basic input validation. The application layer is the heart of the system; it runs the ABAP/Java runtime, processes business logic, and orchestrates everything from a sales order to a payroll run. Finally, the database layer stores all the master and transactional data \u2013 in modern landscapes that\u2019s typically SAP HANA, which gives us real\u2011time analytics and sub\u2011second query response. For example, in my last project we migrated a legacy ECC system to S/4HANA and saw order\u2011to\u2011cash cycle times drop from 48\u202fhours to under 4\u202fhours, largely thanks to the in\u2011memory database handling the heavy lifting.",
        "visual_coaching": ""
      }
